Prebuilt eBPF header
====================

This directory holds a pre-generated C header that embeds the compiled BPF
object as a byte array, together with its source checksum and a shared
license header used during generation.  All files are committed to the
repository so that CI/CD environments and platforms without a clang toolchain
can still build and install the feature without any binary blobs.

Why embed as a C byte array instead of committing tcp_hello.bpf.o?
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Committing ELF binaries to a source tree causes several problems:

  - Diff tools and code-review systems cannot show meaningful diffs for
    binary files, making BPF changes invisible in pull requests.
  - Some CI/CD pipelines and security scanners reject or flag ELF binaries
    in source trees.
  - The byte-array header (tcp_hello_bpf_o.h) is a plain text file: it
    diffs cleanly, is human-inspectable, and carries a proper license header.

Why prebuilt instead of building clang-native via Yocto?
---------------------------------------------------------
  - clang-native is not a default package in most RDKB platform layers.
    Adding it to DEPENDS causes "Nothing PROVIDES 'clang-native'" failures
    on platforms that don't carry the recipe.
  - Even when the recipe exists, compiling LLVM/Clang from source takes
    2-4 hours per build server run -- prohibitive for routine image builds.
  - tcp_hello.bpf.c changes infrequently (BPF logic or kernel struct layout
    changes only).

The prebuilt pattern is standard practice for BPF objects in production
(used by the Linux kernel itself for tools/bpf/ test objects).

Files
-----
  tcp_hello_bpf_o.h   — BPF ELF bytes embedded as a C byte array.
                         Included directly by tcp_loader.c; no separate .bpf.o
                         file is needed at runtime.
                         Generated by the Makefile from tcp_hello.bpf.o using:
                           od -v -An -tx1 tcp_hello.bpf.o | awk '...'
                         The intermediate .bpf.o is deleted after embedding.

  SHA256SUMS          — SHA-256 checksums of the four BPF source files
                         (tcp_hello.bpf.c, bpf_net_defs.h, skbuff_offset.h,
                         tcp_rtt.h).  Compared at build time to warn when
                         sources have changed but the prebuilt has not been
                         regenerated.

  LICENSE_HEADER      — Apache-2.0 license comment block prepended to
                         tcp_hello_bpf_o.h during generation.  Single source
                         of truth for the license text; not a C header.

Developer workflow (system clang, not Yocto clang-native)
----------------------------------------------------------
Use the clang already installed on your development machine.  The Makefile
checks [ -x "$(CLANG)" ] and uses it if available.

  Prerequisites:
    clang >= 10  (check: clang --version)
    kernel source + build trees for the target platform

  Standalone (developer machine):
    cd source/LatencyMeasurement/ebpf
    make CLANG=clang \
         KERNEL_SRC=/path/to/kernel/source \
         KERNEL_BUILD=/path/to/kernel/build \
         LIBBPF_INC=/path/to/libbpf/include \
         LIBBPF_LIB=/path/to/libbpf/lib

  Yocto dev VM (if STAGING variables are already set in the environment):
    bitbake test-and-diagnostic -C compile

  The build automatically:
    1. Compiles tcp_hello.bpf.c -> tcp_hello.bpf.o  (clang -target bpf)
    2. Embeds tcp_hello.bpf.o into tcp_hello_bpf_o.h  (od | awk)
    3. Copies tcp_hello_bpf_o.h -> prebuilt/tcp_hello_bpf_o.h
    4. Regenerates prebuilt/SHA256SUMS from the four source files
    5. Deletes the intermediate tcp_hello.bpf.o

  Commit the updated files:
    git add source/LatencyMeasurement/ebpf/tcp_hello.bpf.c   # if logic changed
    git add source/LatencyMeasurement/ebpf/prebuilt/tcp_hello_bpf_o.h
    git add source/LatencyMeasurement/ebpf/prebuilt/SHA256SUMS
    git commit -m "ebpf: update prebuilt BPF byte-array header"

  To regenerate SHA256SUMS manually (e.g. after copying a prebuilt from
  another machine without going through the full Makefile build):
    cd source/LatencyMeasurement/ebpf
    sha256sum tcp_hello.bpf.c bpf_net_defs.h skbuff_offset.h tcp_rtt.h \
        > prebuilt/SHA256SUMS

Platform without clang (CI/CD, restricted build environments)
--------------------------------------------------------------
The Makefile detects that clang is unavailable (or KERNEL_SRC is not set)
and falls back to copying prebuilt/tcp_hello_bpf_o.h to the build directory.
SHA256SUMS is verified at this point:

  [eBPF] VERIFY  prebuilt SHA256SUMS OK -- sources unchanged
    => prebuilt is fresh; safe to use.

  [eBPF] WARNING: prebuilt/SHA256SUMS mismatch
    => sources were changed after the last prebuilt was generated.
    => The image will still build using the existing prebuilt, but the
       installed BPF object may not reflect the current source.
    => A developer with system clang should regenerate and commit.

Re-compilation triggers
-----------------------
Regenerate the prebuilt whenever any of these change:
  - tcp_hello.bpf.c          (BPF program logic)
  - bpf_net_defs.h            (network struct / map type definitions)
  - skbuff_offset.h           (sk_buff->data offset computation)
  - tcp_rtt.h                 (shared event struct)
  - Kernel config change      (may shift SKBUFF_DATA_OFFSET)
  - Kernel version upgrade    (verify SKBUFF_DATA_OFFSET via runtime scan first)

    => A developer with system clang should regenerate and commit
       the prebuilt.

Re-compilation triggers
-----------------------
Regenerate the prebuilt whenever any of these change:
  - tcp_hello.bpf.c          (BPF program logic)
  - bpf_net_defs.h            (network struct definitions)
  - skbuff_offset.h           (sk_buff->data offset computation)
  - tcp_rtt.h                 (shared event struct)
  - Kernel config change      (may shift SKBUFF_DATA_OFFSET)
  - Kernel version upgrade    (verify SKBUFF_DATA_OFFSET via scan tool first)
